{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"df124bbc-32e9-45f5-918f-9a76da7cc9a7","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Radians measure an angle by comparing its intercepted arc length with the circle’s radius. Start with a circle of radius r.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Radians and arc length"},{"op":"figure","id":"circlefig","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-2.6,2.8],"y":[-2.6,2.8],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"rim","center":[0,0],"r":2},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0],"label":"O"},{"kind":"point","id":"start","at":[2,0]},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"start"},"label":"r"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"df124bbc-32e9-45f5-918f-9a76da7cc9a7","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s2"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"df124bbc-32e9-45f5-918f-9a76da7cc9a7","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The angle intercepts this arc. Its radian measure is arc length divided by radius, so arc length equals radius times angle.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"arcwork","on":"circlefig","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"finish","at":[0,2]},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"finish"}},{"kind":"arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":2.12,"start":0,"end":90,"label":"s"},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"center"},"from":{"at":"start"},"to":{"at":"finish"},"r":0.22,"label":"θ"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"definition","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"\\theta=\\frac{s}{r}\\qquad s=r\\theta"},{"op":"text","id":"condition","anchor":{"el":"definition","side":"below","gap":0.08},"content":"Use radians for the angle."}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"df124bbc-32e9-45f5-918f-9a76da7cc9a7","step":{"id":"s3","script":"For radius two meters and a quarter-turn, the angle is pi over two radians. Multiplying gives an arc length of pi meters.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"values","on":"circlefig","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1,-0.4],"text":"2 m"},{"kind":"text","at":[0.65,0.65],"text":"π/2 rad"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.8,1.8],"text":"π m"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"example","anchor":{"el":"condition","side":"below","gap":0.14},"latex":"s=2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=\\pi\\,\\mathrm{m}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the radius doubles while the angle stays the same, what happens to the arc length?","expected_gist":"The arc length doubles because s = rθ with θ fixed."}}}
